## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="font-size: 0.8em; opacity: 0.8;">[Insert Theme #]</span> – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

## <div style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.5em; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Identification</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Healthy Food for a Balanced Diet</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze articles on general interest topics. Reading: Identify the main topic and related ideas in a simple structured text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze articles about balanced diets by identifying key nutritional information (e.g., "The article mentions that fiber and protein are essential for digestion and for keeping us full.") <br>Can identify the main topic and related ideas in texts on healthy eating (e.g., "Explain the main topic and two benefits of eating balanced meals that include Panamanian staples, such as rice, guandú, and fish.") <br>Can analyze articles on food choices and their health impacts (e.g., "The article explains why whole grains are good for us. They help with digestion and give us energy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## <div style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.5em; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays 6-8 flashcards (or projected images) showing common foods (e.g., apple, broccoli, whole-grain bread, soda, candy bar, chicken). Students work in pairs to quickly classify them as "healthy" or "unhealthy" and justify their choices using simple sentences. This activates vocabulary (healthy, unhealthy, fruits, vegetables, grains) and concepts from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "nutrition," "metabolism," "carbohydrates," and "digestion," focusing on stress in multisyllabic words. For example, "nu-TRI-tion." The teacher also models how to identify key information when reading a short sentence, for instance, by underlining the main noun and verb. The teacher uses a simple sentence like "Eating fruits provides vitamins." and points out 'fruits' as the food and 'vitamins' as the benefit.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'nutrition' a verb or a noun?" (Expected answer: Noun)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'You should eat more vegetables,' am I giving advice or asking a question?" (Expected answer: Giving advice)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational article titled "Fueling Your Body: Why a Balanced Diet Matters" (simple, structured text, A2.4 level). The article incorporates target vocabulary (nutrition, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, grains, protein, fiber, vitamins, minerals, energy, digestion) and grammar (modal "should," first conditional, adverbial clauses for justification).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student understands information in simple texts about general interest topics, such as nutrition and healthy habits.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly (skim) to answer: "What is the main topic of this article?" (Expected answer: The importance of a balanced diet/healthy eating).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 T/F statements related to the article's general content. E.g., "Eating fruits and vegetables helps you stay healthy." (True). "Processed foods are always beneficial for digestion." (False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match sections of the article to headings, e.g., "Section A: What are good carbohydrates?" "Section B: Benefits of Protein."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific details, identifying main ideas and supporting evidence. Using target grammar (modal "should," first conditional, adverbial clauses for justification) and vocabulary (nutrition, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, grains, protein, fiber, vitamins, minerals, nutrients, digestion, healthy, improve, consume) in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-Fill/Cloze Activity (Controlled):</b> Students re-read the article and complete sentences with missing target vocabulary words (e.g., "Fiber and ______ are important for strong muscles.") or grammatical structures (e.g., "You ______ eat a variety of foods for good nutrition."). Teacher monitors and provides support, modeling pronunciation of minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in "bit" (vitamin) and /iː/ in "beat" (fiber) as they read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion based on the text (Semi-controlled):</b> Students complete sentences based on information explicitly stated in the article, focusing on justification. E.g., "Good nutrition is important because..." (They find the answer in the text). "If you consume enough fruits, you will..." (They complete the first conditional sentence based on the text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Identifying Main Ideas and Supporting Details (Pair Check):</b> In pairs, students re-read paragraphs 2 and 3 of the article. They identify the main idea of each paragraph and underline two supporting details. They then explain their choices to their partner, e.g., "The main idea of paragraph 2 is about protein's benefits. It says protein helps build muscles and keeps us full." (Using pragmatic functions of explaining benefits).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading Analysis and Information Synthesis (Graphic Organizer).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a different short, simple article or infographic excerpt (A2.4 level) about a specific aspect of healthy eating (e.g., "The Power of Hydration," "Benefits of Whole Grains," "Superfoods for Teens").</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read their assigned text collaboratively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Together, they complete a "Nutrition Summary Card" graphic organizer. This card asks them to:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify the <b>Main Topic</b> of the article.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">List <b>Two Key Benefits</b> mentioned in the text (e.g., "It improves digestion because it has fiber.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provide <b>One Piece of Advice</b> from the text (e.g., "You should drink eight glasses of water daily for hydration.").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents their summary card to another group, explaining what they learned from their text, using phrases like "Our article explains that if you drink water, you will have more energy" and "The text says we should eat more fruits because they provide vitamins." This also allows for sociolinguistic practice in discussing healthy eating.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "Nutrition Summary Card" graphic organizers completed during the Production stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Main Topic:</b> Was the main topic correctly identified?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Accuracy of Key Benefits:</b> Were the benefits accurately extracted and explained from the text?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Did students use vocabulary words like "nutrition," "benefits," "digestion," "vitamins," "hydration," "protein" appropriately in their summary?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Grammar:</b> Did students correctly use "should" for advice and first conditional/adverbial clauses for justification when presenting/summarizing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did all group members contribute to completing the summary card (observed during the activity)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new piece of nutritional information I learned from reading today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was (easy/difficult) for me to identify the main ideas because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did I use today to understand the articles (e.g., skimming, scanning, underlining key words)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding these articles help me make healthier food choices in my daily life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in helping students analyze articles and identify key nutritional information? (e.g., the graphic organizer, the step-by-step reading tasks)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to identify main ideas or supporting details in the texts? (e.g., pre-teaching more vocabulary, sentence stems for summaries)."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information and vocabulary they have read and analyzed today to discuss healthy food choices and provide dietary advice in conversations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>